                <pb facs="0029" n="22" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg22"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/13965: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-02-06T11:00:00+08:00">February 6, 1937—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 6—10:10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>63. It is learned from a reliable source that factional fighting persists in
                    Sian. A circular telegram sent out by the leading generals <pb facs="0030"
                        n="23" xml:id="pg23"/> in Sian including Yang Hu Cheng and Yu Hsueh Chung on
                    February 5 said that Northeastern and Shensi troops would be partially withdrawn
                    from the Sian region in conformity with the Government’s orders but insisted
                    General Chang Hsueh-liang should return to address and reassure the troops.
                    Informant thought Yu Hsueh Chung was still prevented from returning to Kansu.
                    The trend of informant’s statements implied that the two generals named above
                    were prevented from coming to an agreement with the Government by the sentiment
                    prevailing among the military forces in Sian.</p>
                <p>Sent to the Department, Peiping, and Hankow.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
